      "problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4})$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4})=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-\\frac{3x}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3x/4}+{e}^{-(-3x/4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3x/4}+{e}^{3x/4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
